JIT: no suspend check at the entry of a block a call returns to

Every entry point of a multiblock stores the block header (adr, str) and then checks the suspend
doorbell (ldr, cbz, brk #0xCAFE). The block a call in the same multiblock returns to is an entry point
too, so each call paid the check twice: at the callee's entry and again on the way back. The return site
keeps the header store (the callee replaced it) but drops the check: the callee's entry just made one,
and every way to loop back to the return site still passes a function entry or a backward branch, which
keep theirs.

FBW A380X systems benchmark (squark tools/wasm-bench, 17,900 calls per 33 ms tick): 2.758M -> 2.727M host
instructions per tick (-1.1%); the time per tick did not change measurably.
